# @openbooks/web build environment.
#
# Vite inlines VITE_* variables into the bundle at build time, so nothing here is a
# runtime setting: changing one means rebuilding. It is also all public — the bundle is
# served to anybody — which is why the only variable is an address. No secret, no session
# token, and no database anything belongs in this file. The server's environment is
# documented in the repository root's .env.example.
#
# Copy to packages/web/.env.local for a local override; .env.local is gitignored.
#
# Every variable is declared in src/vite-env.d.ts and resolved in src/env.ts, which is the
# only file in this package that reads the environment (the same rule OB-003 applies to the
# server).

# Where the API lives. Empty — the default — means same-origin, which is the supported
# deployment and not merely the convenient one:
#
#   dev        `yarn workspace @openbooks/web dev` proxies /v1 and /health to
#              http://localhost:3000 (see vite.config.ts), so leave this unset.
#   self-host  serve the built bundle and the API from one hostname behind one reverse
#              proxy, and leave this unset.
#
# Set it only when the API genuinely lives at another origin. Two things must then also be
# true, and neither is true today:
#
#   1. The API must send CORS headers including `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`
#      and an allowlisted `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. The server ships no CORS layer,
#      so a credentialed cross-origin request is refused by the browser. `Idempotency-Key`
#      is not a CORS-safelisted header either, so every write also needs a preflight.
#   2. The session cookie must reach that origin. It is HttpOnly/SameSite=Lax, so the API
#      must be same-site — a sibling subdomain of the app, with the server's
#      SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN set to the shared parent. A different registrable domain
#      cannot carry the session at all under SameSite=Lax.
#
# The hosted layout in infra/terraform/modules/edge/ is a split origin (CloudFront for the
# bundle, a separate api_fqdn for the API), so this is a real prerequisite for it rather
# than a hypothetical.
#
# Accepted forms: empty, an http(s) origin with an optional path prefix
# (https://api.example.com, https://example.com/api), or a root-relative prefix (/api).
# No query string and no fragment. Anything else fails at startup rather than sending
# requests somewhere unintended.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=
